POSSIBLE CONFLICT WITH THE AUDIT-REGISTER RECONCILIATION — flagging so whoever plans this can settle it rather than have two records disagree silently.

Today I triaged the product audit register (task-963cf1d125134c6193b7af0e53deeac3) and recorded its item 5 — "MCP: the HTTP session registry grows unbounded, and close() never releases daemon-side bindings" — as ALREADY CLOSED, on this measurement at HEAD 192360e:

- packages/mcp/src/http/http-session.ts:222 calls `registry.delete(mcpSessionId)`, a per-session eviction outside close(), which addresses the "grows unbounded" half.
- packages/mcp/src/http/http-server.ts:303-309:
```
async close(): Promise<void> {
  for (const entry of registry.entries()) {
    registry.delete(entry.sessionId);
    await entry.attachment.transport.close();
    await entry.attachment.server.close();
  }
}
```
which deletes every entry and closes both the transport and the server.
- entries() returns a copy (`[...sessions.values()]` at http-session.ts:94-95), so mutating during iteration is safe — it looks like a bug and is not.

That closure is what made the register arithmetic balance at 9 created + 0 declined + 2 already-closed = 11 (comment-f956cc9d992040e295a616b36c5b3ad3).

Two possibilities, and I genuinely do not know which:
(a) "daemon-side bindings" means something BEYOND the MCP adapter's transport and server — bindings held on the daemon side of the connection that close() does not reach. If so my reading was too narrow, this task is real, and the register should move to 8 created + 0 declined + 3 already-closed... no — to 10 created + 0 declined + 1 already-closed, since item 5 would then be open and this task covers it.
(b) This task was created from the register's original wording rather than from current bytes, in which case it duplicates an item already closed and should be closed as already-fixed.

Please resolve it explicitly during planning: measure what "daemon-side bindings" names on current bytes, and if (a) is right, say so on task-963cf1d1 so its reconciliation is corrected rather than left contradicted. If (b), close this one and cite the measurement above.

I am not touching either task — this is a measurement dispute and whoever plans it should own the answer. Flagging it rather than assuming my reading was right.
